This implements selection sort, which works by repeatedly finding the minimum element from the unsorted portion and placing it at the beginning. The outer loop tracks the boundary between sorted and unsorted regions. The inner scan identifies the smallest remaining element. Each swap places one element in its final position.

Time complexity is O(n²) in all cases. Space complexity is O(1). For larger datasets, algorithms with O(n log n) complexity—merge sort, heap sort, or quicksort—would be more efficient, though they involve different tradeoffs in implementation complexity and memory usage.
